  (text "Power subsystem -- USB-C 5V in, single 3.3V rail (pilink-7el.2)\nRP2350B's internal switched regulator makes its own 1.1V core voltage\nfrom this 3.3V rail (same pattern as stock Pico boards) -- no separate\n1.1V regulator on this sheet. +5V arrives from the USB-C sheet\n(pilink-7el.7); PWR_FLAG marks it as externally driven for ERC." (at 60.96 30.48 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid 05f9001f-68b4-41c9-b633-340747c14553)
  )

  (text "U1: AP2114H-3.3TRG1 (BCD Semiconductor / Diodes Incorporated), SOT-223\n(H package), genuinely CMOS-process LDO -- swapped in for the prior\nAMS1117-3.3 (bipolar NPN-pass) to fix a dropout-margin problem against\nUSB VBUS's worst-case floor (pilink-ddq, round 2). AMS1117's bipolar\ntopology has a near-fixed ~1.2-1.3V dropout offset that barely improves\nbelow its 0.8A guaranteed-spec edge, which left only +35 to -100mV of\nmargin once a tripped F401 PTC fuse (usb_c.kicad_sch) was factored in --\ntoo thin. AP2114H-3.3 is a true CMOS pass-FET device: its own datasheet\n(BCD Semiconductor, Jan 2013 rev, titled \"1A LOW NOISE CMOS LDO REGULATOR\nWITH ENABLE\") guarantees Dropout Voltage VDROP @ IOUT=1A = 450mV (typ) /\n750mV (max) in its AP2114-3.3 Electrical Characteristics table, and\nguarantees Maximum Output Current >=1.0A (min) at VOUT=3.25-3.35V -- a\nreal, verified spec, not a marketing figure. Recommended Supply Voltage\n2.5-6.0V (Absolute Max 6.5V) comfortably covers USB VBUS up to ~5.25V.\nLCSC C150716, confirmed via jlcpcb-search MCP 2026-11-15: 10,090 units in\nstock (Extended part). Pin-compatible with the AMS1117 it replaces: SOT-223\n(H) Pin Descriptions table gives 1=GND, 2=VOUT, 3=VIN -- the IDENTICAL\npin-to-pad mapping as AMS1117 on the same SOT-223-3_TabPin2 footprint\n(tab=VOUT=pin2), chosen specifically to minimize layout/decoupling churn\nsince PCB layout (pilink-7el.9) hadn't started yet when this swap was made.\nThe 3-pin H/HA SOT-223 package has no EN pin (EN only exposed on the\nSOIC-8/PSOP-8/ADJ packages) -- always enabled, no extra components needed.\n\nDropout margin vs USB's VBUS floor (resolved, pilink-ddq): worst-case VBUS\nfloor at the connector, direct host connection, no hub: 4.75V (USB-IF\n\"ports supplying >1 unit load\" minimum) - 0.125V (spec's max cable IR\ndrop, VBUSD ECN) = 4.625V. Our load's own worst-case peak is ~0.65A (see\ncurrent-budget note above); AP2114H-3.3's dropout scales ~linearly with\nload current for a CMOS pass FET (Vdrop ~= Iout x Rds_on), so scaling the\n1A-rated spec down to 0.65A: ~293mV typ / ~488mV max. F401\n(usb_c.kicad_sch) adds its own series resistance: 40mOhm normal (26mV\ndrop @ 0.65A) or 230mOhm post-trip (150mV drop @ 0.65A).\nRealistic case (typ dropout, F401 normal): required Vin = 3.3V + 0.293V =\n3.593V; available Vin = 4.625V - 0.026V = 4.599V; margin = +1.01V.\nPessimistic case (max dropout, F401 tripped): required Vin = 3.3V +\n0.488V = 3.788V; available Vin = 4.625V - 0.150V = 4.475V; margin =\n+688mV. Even the fully-pessimistic, non-scaled case (full 750mV max\ndropout at the 1A rated current, despite our 0.65A load, stacked with a\ntripped F401): required Vin = 3.3V + 0.75V = 4.05V; margin = 4.475V -\n4.05V = +425mV. All three cases are comfortably positive -- an order of\nmagnitude more margin than AMS1117's +35 to -100mV, even before counting\nJ401 connector contact resistance or PCB trace resistance (unquantified,\nbut each worth well under 100mV at this current, so they don't threaten\nany of these margins). Verify at bring-up with a real meter under\nworst-case load, same as this sheet's other flagged assumptions." (at 60.96 46.99 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid 7e9033c0-519c-4359-a2c3-9f2f753fbe3f)
  )

  (text "Decoupling: C1 10uF bulk on the regulator input, C2 22uF on the output\n(both ceramic, 0805) -- unchanged from the prior AMS1117 wiring. AP2114H-3.3's\nown datasheet (see Datasheet link above) lists its stability test condition\nas CIN=COUT=4.7uF and its own feature list says \"Stable with 4.7uF Flexible\nCap: Ceramic, Tantalum and Aluminum Electrolytic\" -- i.e. ceramic is\nexplicitly fine (unlike AMS1117, which wanted 22uF solid tantalum on the\noutput for guaranteed stability). C1/C2 both already exceed this part's\n4.7uF minimum, so no value change was needed for the swap, and the prior\nceramic-vs-tantalum bring-up-verify flag is resolved: this part's own\ndatasheet confirms ceramic is a supported cap type, not just a cost/size\ntradeoff to re-check. C3 100nF local ceramic at the 3V3 point-of-load is\nstandard LDO-output practice, not a guide-mandated value -- RP-008280-DS-2\nRel. 3 Sec. 2.2.1 only covers RP2350B's own pins, which live on\npilink-7el.3." (at 60.96 160.02 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid 54487828-8fea-454b-a628-566a5c551201)
  )

  (text "Out of scope for this sheet (lives on the RP2350B + Memory sheet,\npilink-7el.3): RP2350B's ~11 per-pin 100nF VDD3V3/IOVDD decouplers, the\nVREG_LX/VREG_AVDD inductor+caps for its internal 3.3V->1.1V core\nregulator, and the 10uF bulk cap (C19 in the reference design) on +3V3\nnear the RP2350 -- this project splits what Raspberry Pi's single-sheet\nreference design combines, across this sheet and the RP2350B+memory one." (at 60.96 185.42 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid 4f509b76-bf21-47f2-b154-7483f0eb276e)
  )
)
